##3. QUESTION: KYA HOGA AGAR UPDATE OPERATION MEIN PARTITIONING KEY KI VALUE HI CHANGE HO JAAYE?
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Agar Update Operation Partitioning Key ki value change karta hai, to Row ka target Partition bhi change ho sakta hai. 
Aisi situation mein Row ko ek Partition se doosre Partition mein move karna pad sakta hai.

Isliye Partitioning Key ke liye generally stable aur rarely-changing Column choose karna preferable hota hai, 
taaki frequent Row movement avoid kiya ja sake.






============================================================================================================
#6. WRITE-INTENSIVE APPLICATION KO KAISE SCALE KAREIN?   - SHARDING (DISTRIBUTED HORIZONTAL PARTITIONING)
============================================================================================================

Read Replicas se reads multiple nodes ke across distribute ho gaye.
Lekin writes abhi bhi Primary DB par hi ja rahi hain.

Agar write traffic significantly increase ho jaaye, to saari writes ek hi
Primary DB ko handle karni padengi, aur woh eventually write bottleneck ban sakta hai.


-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
## QUESTION: KYA HUM WRITES KO BHI MULTIPLE DATABASE NODES PAR DISTRIBUTE KAR SAKTE HAIN?
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Suppose hum multiple Write Databases bana dein aur randomly
different write requests ko different database nodes par bhejne lagen:

                Application
                     |
          +----------+----------+
          |          |          |
          v          v          v
        WRITE DB-1  WRITE DB-2  WRITE DB-3


Problem ye hai ki agar same logical data ke writes different databases
par independently jaane lagen, to data consistency maintain karna
difficult ho sakta hai.


## EXAMPLE :
------------

Initial User state:

user_id = 101
name = Anurag

WRITE 1 → WRITE DB-1

UPDATE User
SET name = 'Anand'
WHERE user_id = 101;

WRITE 2 → WRITE DB-2

UPDATE User
SET name = 'Anuj'
WHERE user_id = 101;

Ab dono databases ne same user ke liye apni independent history
maintain kar li:

WRITE DB-1:

    Anurag → Anand


WRITE DB-2:

    Anurag → Anuj


Ideally, agar dono writes ek hi database par sequentially
execute hui hoti, to data history kuch aisi honi chahiye thi:

Anurag → Anand → Anuj

Lekin yahan same user ka data different database nodes par
independently update hua hai.

Ab Application ke liye ye determine karna difficult ho sakta hai ki:

"user_id = 101 ke liye correct/latest data kis database mein hai?"

Isliye writes ko multiple database nodes ke across distribute karne ke liye
humein ek deterministic routing rule chahiye.

Yaani system ko reliably pata hona chahiye:

"Application ko pata hona chahiye ki particular data kis Database node par store karna hai,
taaki future mein jab usi data ke liye read/write request aaye, to us request ko usi Database node par route kiya ja sake."

Isi concept ko Sharding solve karta hai.



--------------------------------
## QUESTION: SHARDING KYA HAI?
--------------------------------

Sharding mein data ko Rows ke basis par horizontally divide karke independent database nodes (Shards)
ke across store kiya jaata hai. Har Shard total dataset ka sirf ek subset store aur manage karta hai.

Application ke andar ya Application ke saamne/saath koi routing mechanism,
defined routing rule ke basis par decide karta hai ki particular data kis Shard mein store hoga.


## EXMAPLE:
-------------


                Application
                     |
                     v
              Shard Routing Logic
                     |
         +-----------+-----------+
         |           |           |
         v           v           v
     Shard 1      Shard 2      Shard 3


Suppose total users:

User 1 → User 1,000,000


Sharding ke baad:

Shard 1 → Users 1 - 333,333
Shard 2 → Users 333,334 - 666,666
Shard 3 → Users 666,667 - 1,000,000

Yaani har Shard total dataset ka sirf ek portion handle/store karta hai, 
jisse data aur write load multiple database nodes ke across distribute ho sakta hai.

Isse overall system ki total write capacity increase ho sakti hai.



---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
## QUESTION: APPLICATION KO KAISE PATA CHALTA HAI KI REQUEST KO KAUNSE SHARD PAR ROUTE KARNA HAI? — SHARD KEY
---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Shard Key woh field/attribute hota hai jiske basis par system decide karta hai ki particular data 
kis Shard mein store hoga aur kis Shard se retrieve kiya jayega.

- Write/Update: Request correct Shard par route hoti hai.
- Read: System correct Shard identify karke data retrieve karta hai.


Shard Key request ke time automatically discover nahi hoti.

Application design ke time pehle se define kiya jaata hai ki
particular Entity/Table ke liye kaunsi field Shard Key hogi.


## REQUEST FLOW
-----------------

Client Request
      |
      v
  Controller
      |
      v
Service Layer
      |
      | User Entity ke liye
      | Shard Key = user_id
      v
Extract user_id
      |
      v
Apply Shard Routing Rule
      |
      v
Correct Shard



## EXAMPLE
-------------

Maan lo:

User Table → Shard Key = user_id

User:

user_id = 101
name = Anurag
email = anurag@example.com


## WRITE
----------

New User create karte time, Application user_id ko Shard Key ke roop mein use karke 
Shard Routing Rule apply karegi aur User ko corresponding Shard mein store karegi.


## FUTURE UPDATE
-------------------

Future mein same User ke liye Update request aane par, Application dobara 
user_id ko Shard Key ke roop mein use karke same Shard Routing Rule apply karegi.

Isliye particular user_id ke related writes consistently same Shard par route hote hain.



----------------------------------------------------------------
## QUESTION: SHARD KEY CHOOSE KARTE TIME KYA DEKHNA CHAHIYE ? 
----------------------------------------------------------------

1. DATA ACCESS PATTERN
------------------------

Sabse pehle ye dekhna chahiye ki kisi particular Entity ka data
application ki most requests mein usually kis field ke basis par
access kiya jaata hai.

Maan lo User entity ke liye most requests:

GET /users/{user_id}
UPDATE /users/{user_id}
DELETE /users/{user_id}

In requests mein user_id directly available hai.

Isliye hum:

User Table → Shard Key = user_id

choose kar sakte hain.

Ab Application user_id ke basis par Shard Routing Rule apply karke
correct Shard identify kar sakti hai.


## LEKIN AGAR REQUEST MEIN SHARD KEY AVAILABLE NA HO TO?
-----------------------------------------------------------

Maan lo occasionally ek request aati hai:

GET /users?email=anurag@example.com

Is request mein Application ke paas email hai,
lekin directly user_id available nahi hai.

Agar user_id Shard Key hai, to Application directly
correct Shard identify nahi kar sakta.

Aise case mein commonly kuch approaches use ki ja sakti hain.

1. SCATTER-GATHER QUERY : 
--------------------------- 

   Application request ko multiple, ya zarurat padne par
   all Shards par bhej sakti hai.

   Har Shard par query execute ho sakti hai:

   Find User WHERE email = anurag@example.com

   Jis Shard mein required User milta hai,
   wahan se result return kiya ja sakta hai.

   Jab correct Shard directly identify nahi kiya ja sakta aur query ko multiple
   Shards par execute karna padta hai, to woh Cross-Shard Query ka ek case hota hai.

   ## PROBLEM 
   ------------
   Ek logical request multiple Shards ko hit kar sakti hai.
   Isliye frequent queries ke liye Scatter-Gather expensive ho sakta hai 
   aur generally avoid karna preferable hota hai.


2. LOOKUP / DIRECTORY SERVICE
-------------------------------

   Ek separate lookup/directory mapping maintain ki ja sakti hai:

   email                       user_id      
   --------------------------------------
   anurag@example.com          101        

   Is approach mein Application pehle email ke basis par corresponding user_id
   retrieve karti hai.

   Uske baad user_id ko Shard Key ke roop mein use karke
   Shard Routing Rule apply kiya jaata hai aur correct Shard
   identify kiya jaata hai.



2. DATA DISTRIBUTION
----------------------
  
  Shard Key aisi honi chahiye jisse data aur request traffic
  multiple Shards ke across reasonably distribute ho sake.

  ## EXAMPLE:
  ------------

  Shard Key = country

  India Users  → Very High
  US Users     → High
  Others       → Low

  Agar majority users India se hain, to India-related data ek hi Shard par bahut zyada accumulate ho sakta hai.
  to us Shard par disproportionatel zyada data aur requests aa sakti hain.

  ## RESULT:
  -----------

    Uneven Data / Traffic Distribution
            ↓
      One Shard receives
    significantly more load
            ↓
         Hot Shard
            ↓
        Bottleneck


## IMPORTANT
--------------

Koi universally perfect Shard Key nahi hoti.

Shard Key choose karte time application ke Data Access Patterns aur
Data/Traffic Distribution dono consider karne chahiye.

Agar Shard Key suitable nahi hui, to future mein:

- Uneven Load
- Hot Shards
- Scaling Bottlenecks

jaise problems aa sakti hain.



---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
## QUESTION: SHARD KEY KE BASIS PAR SYSTEM KAISE DECIDE KARTA HAI KI DATA KAUNSE SHARD PAR JAAYEGA? — SHARD ROUTING STRATEGIES
---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Shard Key identify karne ke baad, system ko ek routing strategy ki zarurat hoti hai
jiske basis par Shard Key ki value ko kisi specific Shard se map kiya ja sake.

Common Shard Routing Strategies:

1. Range-Based Sharding
2. Hash-Based Sharding
3. Consistent Hashing
4. Directory / Lookup-Based Sharding



1. RANGE-BASED SHARDING
-------------------------

Range-Based Sharding mein Shard Key ki values ko different
predefined ranges mein divide kiya jaata hai.

Har range ko ek particular Shard assign kiya jaata hai.


Maan lo:

Shard Key = user_id

Aur user_id Shard Key ke liye ranges kuch aise define ki gayi hain:


Shard 1:

    user_id → 1 to 333,333


Shard 2:

    user_id → 333,334 to 666,666


Shard 3:

    user_id → 666,667 to 1,000,000


Request ki Shard Key value jis predefined range mein aati hai,
request us corresponding Shard par route ki jaati hai.



## RANGE-BASED SHARDING KA MAJOR PROBLEM — HOT SHARD
--------------------------------------------------------

Range-Based Sharding mein Shard Key ki values ko predefined ranges mein
divide kiya jaata hai aur har range ek particular Shard ko assign hoti hai.

Agar kisi particular range ka data disproportionately zyada ho jaaye ya us range ke data 
ke liye requests disproportionately zyada aane lagen, to us range ko handle karne wale Shard
par baaki Shards ke comparison mein significantly zyada load aa sakta hai.

Aise disproportionately overloaded Shard ko Hot Shard kehte hain.

## EXAMPLE 1:
---------------

Shard 1 → user_id 1 to 333,333
Shard 2 → user_id 333,334 to 666,666
Shard 3 → user_id 666,667 to 1,000,000

Ab maan lo user_id sequentially generate ho raha hai

Isliye naye User creation requests aur unse se related
write (Update) traffic disproportionately Shard 1 par aa sakta hai,
jabki baaki Shards comparatively kam utilized ya idle ho sakte hain.

Aisi situation mein ek Shard baaki Shards ke comparison mein
significantly zyada load handle karne lagta hai and hot shard ban jata hai.


## EXAMPLE 2:
--------------

Shard Key = country

Agar:

India   → Very High Traffic
US      → Medium Traffic
Others  → Low Traffic


Agar India-related data ke liye Write Requests bahut high hain,
to India ki Shard Key value ke corresponding Shard par
baaki Shards ke comparison mein disproportionately zyada
Write Traffic aa sakta hai.

Jabki baaki Shards comparatively kam Write Traffic handle kar
rahe ho sakte hain.

Is wajah se India-related data ko handle karne wala Shard
disproportionately zyada load handle karne lag sakta hai
and hot shard ban sakta hai.



## QUESTION: HOT SHARD KO HANDLE KAISE KIYA JA SAKTA HAI? - REBALANCING
--------------------------------------------------------------------------

Agar kisi particular Shard par disproportionately zyada write
traffic aa raha hai, to us Shard ki existing range ko split kiya
ja sakta hai.

Us range ke data ka ek portion naye ya comparatively less loaded
Shard mein move kiya ja sakta hai.

Is process ko Rebalancing kehte hain.


## EXAMPLE:
-------------

Predefined user_id ranges:

Shard 1 → user_id 1 to 100
Shard 2 → user_id 101 to 200
Shard 3 → user_id 201 to 300


Maan lo kisi reason se Shard 3 par disproportionately zyada
traffic aa raha hai:

Shard 1 → 20,000 requests/sec
Shard 2 → 25,000 requests/sec
Shard 3 → 90,000 requests/sec


Is situation mein Shard 3 ki existing range ko split kiya
ja sakta hai aur uske data ka ek portion naye Shard mein
move kiya ja sakta hai.


Before:

Shard 3 → user_id 201 to 300


After:

Shard 3 → user_id 201 to 250

Shard 4 → user_id 251 to 300


Is process mein user_id 251 to 300 ke existing data ko
Shard 3 se Shard 4 mein migrate/redistribute karna padega.

Saath hi, Shard Routing configuration ko bhi update karna padega,
taaki:

user_id 201 to 250 → Shard 3

user_id 251 to 300 → Shard 4


Isi process ko Rebalancing kehte hain.

Lekin Rebalancing process potentially expensive aur operationally
complex ho sakti hai, kyunki existing data migrate karna aur
routing configuration update karni pad sakti hai.




2. HASH-BASED SHARDING
-------------------------

Hash-Based Sharding mein Shard Key ki value par ek Hash Function apply kiya jaata hai.

Hash Function ka output use karke system decide karta hai ki data kis Shard par store ya retrieve kiya jayega.


## EXAMPLE:
------------

Maan lo:

Shard Key = user_id

Total Shards = 3


Routing rule:

hash(user_id) % Number of Shards


## BENEFIT
------------

Hash-Based Sharding Shard Key values ko multiple Shards ke across
relatively evenly distribute karne mein help karta hai.

Isliye sequential Shard Key values bhi different Shards par
distribute ho sakti hain, jisse Hot Shard ka risk reduce ho sakta hai.

Example:

user_id = 101 → Hash → Shard 2
user_id = 102 → Hash → Shard 0
user_id = 103 → Hash → Shard 1
user_id = 104 → Hash → Shard 2


## IMPORTANT:
--------------

Hash-Based Sharding Hot Shard problem ko completely eliminate nahi karti,
lekin generally Range-Based Sharding ke comparison mein
data aur load ko more evenly distribute karne mein help karti hai.


## LIMITATION
---------------

Hash-Based Sharding ki major limitation tab aati hai jab
cluster mein Shards ki total number change hoti hai.

Maan lo :

Routing Rule:

hash(shard_key) % number of shards

Initially:

Total Shards = 3

Ab system ko scale karne ke liye ek naya Shard add kiya:

Total Shards = 4

Ab same `user_id` ke liye routing result change ho sakta hai.

Example:

user_id = 101

Initially:

101 % 3 = 2

So:

user_id = 101 → Shard 2


Isliye `user_id = 101` ka data initially Shard 2 mein stored hai.

Ab ek naya Shard add karne ke baad routing rule change ho jaata hai:

101 % 4 = 1

Ab:

user_id = 101 → Shard 1

Yahan problem ye hai ki user_id = 101 ka existing data abhi bhi
Shard 2 mein stored hai.

Lekin naya Routing Rule apply karne ke baad future requests ke liye:

user_id = 101 → Shard 1

route calculate hoga.

Isliye future mein user_id = 101 ke liye aane wali Write (Update / Delete) requests,
Shard 1 par route ho sakti hain, jabki existing data abhi bhi Shard 2 mein present hai.

Yaani Routing Rule aur actual data location ke beech mismatch create ho jaata hai.

Is problem ko resolve karne ke liye `user_id = 101` ka existing data
Shard 2 se Shard 1 mein migrate karna padega.

Ye data migration expensive aur operationally complex ho sakti hai.

Isliye Shards ki number change hone par potentially large amount of
existing data ko migrate/redistribute karna pad sakta hai.

Isi problem ko efficiently handle karne ke liye
Consistent Hashing use ki ja sakti hai.




3. CONSISTENT HASHING
------------------------

Consistent Hashing bhi Hash-Based Sharding ki tarah Shard Key ke basis par data 
ko multiple Shards ke across distribute karne mein help karta hai.
Lekin iska main advantage ye hai ki jab Database Cluster mein Shards add ya remove hote hain,
to Hash-Based Sharding ke comparison mein relatively kam existing data ko migrate karna padta hai.


## QUESTION: CONSISTENT HASHING KAISE WORK KARTI HAI ?
-------------------------------------------------------

Consistent Hashing mein:

- Shard Nodes ko bhi hash kiya jaata hai.
- Shard Key value ko bhi hash kiya jaata hai.
- Dono ko ek fixed Hash Ring par place kiya jaata hai.
- Uske baad Shard Key ki position se clockwise direction mein jo
  pehla Shard Node milta hai, data us Shard ko assign hota hai.


## QUESTION: HASH SPACE AUR HASH RING KYA HAI ?
--------------------------------------------------

Ek Hash Function kisi input ko ek fixed numeric range ke andar ek value mein
convert karta hai.

hash(input) → 0 to 359 ke beech koi value

Is fixed numeric range ko Hash Space kehte hain.

Real systems mein ye range bahut large hoti hai.

Conceptually, is Hash Space ko circular form mein represent kiya ja sakta hai.

Kyuki Hash Space ko circular form mein represent kiya jaata hai,
ise Hash Ring kehte hain.


## EXMAPLE
------------

Maan lo based on our Hash function hamara Hash Space hai:

0 ------------------------------ 359

Ab hum isko circular form mein represent karte hain:


                    0 / 360
                       |
                       |
          300          |          60
             \         |         /
              \        |        /
               \       |       /
                \      |      /
                 HASH RING
                /      |      \
               /       |       \
              /        |        \
             /         |         \
          240                      120

                       |
                      180


Ab maan lo humare paas 3 Database Shards hain:

Shard 1
Shard 2
Shard 3


STEP 1: SHARDS KO HASH RING PAR PLACE KARNA
-----------------------------------------------

Sabse pehle Shard Nodes ke identifiers ko Hash Function par pass kiya jaata hai.

Conceptually:

hash("Shard-1") → 40
hash("Shard-2") → 150
hash("Shard-3") → 280

Ab ye Shards Hash Ring par unki corresponding positions par place ho jaayenge:


                     0 / 360
                        |
                      Shard 1
                       (40)

          Shard 3                    Shard 2
           (280)                      (150)



STEP 2: SHARDS KEY VALUES KO HASH RING PAR PLACE KARNA
-------------------------------------------------------

Ab maan lo:

Shard Key = user_id

Aur request hai:

user_id = 101

Sabse pehle:

hash(user_id)

calculate hoga.

Maan lo:

hash(101) → 100

Yaani:

user_id = 101

Hash Position = 100

Ab Hash Ring par position 100 locate ki jaayegi.

Shard Nodes ki positions already mapped hain:

Shard 1 → 40
Shard 2 → 150
Shard 3 → 280

Ab position 100 se clockwise direction mein move kiya jaayega.

Position 100 ke baad clockwise direction mein pehla Shard Node hai:

Shard 2 → 150

Isliye:

user_id = 101 → Shard 2

Yaani user_id = 101 ke liye Write request
Shard 2 par route ki jaayegi.


## QUESTION: CONSISTENT HASHING KA BENIFIT ?
-----------------------------------------------

Consistent Hashing ka benefit data migration ko completely
eliminate karna nahi, balki minimize karna hai.

Modulo-Based Hashing mein Shards ki total number change hone par
routing formula change ho jaata hai.

Is wajah se bahut saari existing keys ki mapping change ho sakti hai,
jisse large-scale data migration ki zarurat pad sakti hai.

Lekin Consistent Hashing mein Node/Shards add ya remove hone par
sirf Hash Ring ke locally affected portion ki keys ki mapping change hoti hai.

Isliye sirf affected keys ka data migrate karna padta hai,
jabki Hash Ring ke baaki portion ki key-to-Shard mapping
unchanged rehti hai.


