## QUESTION: DATABASE TRANSACTION KYA HAI?
--------------------------------------------

Database Transaction ek ya multiple Database Operations ka
aisa group hota hai jise Database ek single logical unit of work
ki tarah treat karta hai.

Transaction ke andar included Operations:

- Ya to sabhi Operations successfully complete honge aur
  unke changes permanently commit honge.

- Ya agar Transaction ke execution ke dauran koi Operation fail ho jaaye, 
  to Transaction ke andar pehle successfully execute hue Operations ke 
  changes bhi rollback kar diye jaayenge.


Yaani Transaction ensure karti hai:

All Operations Successful
        ↓
      COMMIT

Any Operation Failed
        ↓
     ROLLBACK


## EXAMPLE
------------

Maan lo Account A se Account B mein ₹1,000 transfer karne hain.

Initial State

- Account A → ₹10,000
- Account B → ₹5,000

Required Operations
- Account A se ₹1,000 deduct karna.
- Account B mein ₹1,000 add karna.


## WITHOUT DATABASE TRANSACTION
--------------------------------

Agar dono Operations independently execute kiye jaayen aur pehla Operation successfully complete ho jaaye:

Account A → ₹9,000

Lekin agar doosra Operation fail ho jaaye, to state ho sakti hai:

Account A → ₹9,000
Account B → ₹5,000

Yaani ₹1,000 source Account se deduct ho gaye, lekin destination Account mein add nahi hue.

Is situation mein Business Operation partially complete hui hai, jiski wajah se Database
ka data expected business-consistent state mein nahi hai.


## WITH DATABASE TRANSACTION
------------------------------

Database Transaction dono related Operations ko ek single logical unit 
of work ki tarah manage karti hai.

Is example mein agar:

Account A se ₹1,000 deduct → SUCCESS

Lekin:

Account B mein ₹1,000 add → FAIL

To Transaction ROLLBACK ho sakti hai.

Final State after ROLLBACK

Account A → ₹10,000
Account B → ₹5,000


Is tarah Transaction partial changes ko permanently persist hone se prevent karne mein help 
karti hai aur Database ko expected consistent state mein maintain karne mein help karti hai.



--------------------------------------------------------------
## QUESTION: EXPLICIT AUR IMPLICIT TRANSACTION KYA HOTA HAI?
--------------------------------------------------------------

Database Transactions generally do tarah se execute ho sakti hain:

- Explicit Transaction
- Auto-Commit Transaction

Dono mein main difference yeh hota hai ki transaction ki boundary (kab shuru hoga aur kab khatam)
kaun decide kar raha hai—database system khud ya fir developer.


1: EXPLICIT TRANSACTION
-------------------------

Explicit Transaction mein Developer khud Transaction ki boundary define karta hai.

## QUESTION : RAW SQL / WORKBENCH MEIN EXPLICIT TRANSACTION KAISE LIKHTE HAIN ?
---------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Ek Transaction ke andar multiple related Database Operations ho sakte hain.

In Operations mein:

- READ Operations
- WRITE Operations

dono include ho sakte hain.


## EXAMPLE
------------

Maan lo Account A se Account B mein ₹1,000 transfer karne hain:

Maan lo Account A se Account B mein ₹1,000 transfer karne hain:

BEGIN TRANSACTION;   

-- Operation 1
UPDATE accounts
SET balance = balance - 1000
WHERE account_id = 101;

-- Operation 2
UPDATE accounts
SET balance = balance + 1000
WHERE account_id = 501;

COMMIT;  


## IMPORTANT COMMANDS :
-------------------------

1: BEGIN TRANSACTION; 
  - Transaction start karta hai.
  - Kuch Databases mein sirf BEGIN; bhi use kiya ja sakta hai. 


2: COMMIT;
  - Transaction ke andar successfully execute hue sabhi Operations ke changes 
    ko permanently Database mein persist karta hai.

ROLLBACK
  - Current Transaction ke andar previously successful Operations ke uncommitted changes ko undo karta hai.
  - Isse Database ka state Transaction start hone se pehle wali consistent state mein wapas aa jaata hai.


### CASE 1 : TRANSACTION KE SABHI OPERATIONS SUCCESSFULLY COMPLETE HO JAAYE
------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Script mein COMMIT statement pehle se likha hone ka matlab yeh nahi hai ki Database
beech mein failure hone ke baad bhi blindly Transaction ko COMMIT kar dega.

Agar Transaction ke previous Operations successfully execute ho jaate hain aur execution 
COMMIT statement tak pahunch jaati hai, tab COMMIT execute hota hai.

COMMIT successfully execute hone ke baad Transaction ke andar kiye gaye uncommitted changes
permanently Database mein persist ho jaate hain.


### CASE 1 : TRANSACTION KE BEECH MEIN KOI OPERATION FAIL HO JAAYE 
--------------------------------------------------------------------

Operation fail hone ke baad normal execution flow aage COMMIT statement 
tak proceed nahi karta, isliye COMMIT execute nahi hota. 

Is situation mein pehle successfully execute hue Operations ke changes
abhi permanently persist nahi hue hote, 

Lekin Transaction automatically abort/rollback hogi ya abhi active rahegi,
ye DBMS aur error ke type par depend karta hai.

Agar Transaction abhi active hai, to Developer explicitly:

ROLLBACK;

execute karke Transaction ke uncommitted changes ko undo kar sakta hai.


## QUESTION : APPLICATION LEVEL MEIN EXPLICIT TRANSACTION KAISE LIKHTE HAIN ?
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Spring Boot mein commonly @Transactional annotation use ki jaati hai.

Spring mein @Transactional transaction management framework ko indicate karta hai
ki annotated method ke execution ke around ek Database Transaction manage karna hai.


Yahan:

@Transactional Method Start
        ↓
Database Transaction Start
        ↓
Operation 1 → Debit Account A
        ↓
Operation 2 → Credit Account B
        ↓
All Operations Successful?
      /             \
    YES              NO
     ↓                ↓
   COMMIT          ROLLBACK




2: AUTO-COMMIT TRANSACTION
---------------------------

Auto-Commit enabled hone par generally har individual SQL statement apni separate
Transaction ki tarah execute hoti hai.

- Statement successful → Automatically COMMIT
- Statement fail → Us Statement ke uncommitted changes Abort / Rollback


## EXAMPLE
------------

Maan lo Auto-Commit enabled hai aur Developer ye query execute karta hai:

UPDATE accounts
SET balance = balance - 1000
WHERE account_id = 101;

Database internally is statement ko ek Transaction ke andar execute karta hai.


                SQL Statement
                      │
                      v
        Database executes Statement
                      │
              ┌───────┴───────┐
              │               │
          SUCCESS            FAIL
              │               │
              v               v
        Auto COMMIT      Abort / Rollback
              │               │
              v               v
     Changes Permanently   Statement ke changes
        Persisted          Persist nahi honge



## IMPORTANT: AUTO-COMMIT MULTIPLE QUERIES KO EK TRANSACTION NAHI BANATA
--------------------------------------------------------------------------

Ye sabse important point hai.

Maan lo Auto-Commit enabled hai:

-- Query 1
UPDATE accounts
SET balance = balance - 1000
WHERE account_id = 101;

-- Query 2
UPDATE accounts
SET balance = balance + 1000
WHERE account_id = 501;


In dono Queries ko Database ek single DB Transaction ka part nahi maanta.
Balki, har Query apni separate Database Transaction mein independently execute
hoti hai aur successful hone par independently Auto-COMMIT ho jaati hai.

Query 1 → Success → Auto-COMMIT

Query 2 → Fail → Abort / Rollback

Query 2 fail hone par Query 1 automatically Rollback nahi hogi,
kyunki Query 1 pehle hi independently COMMIT ho chuki hoti hai.



