## QUESTION: LOCKS KYA HOTE HAIN?
------------------------------------

Database Locks ek Concurrency Control mechanism hain jo multiple concurrent Transactions
ko same data par conflicting operations karne se control karta hai.

## EXAMPLE:
------------

Maan lo Transaction T1 Account A ka Balance update kar rahi hai.

T1 → Acquire Lock on Account A 
↓ 
Update Balance 
↓ 
Release Lock 


Isi time T2 bhi Account A ko update karna chahti hai:

T2 → Wants to Update Account A 
          ↓ 
        WAIT 
          ↓ 
  T1 Releases Lock 
          ↓ 
  T2 Can Proceed

Is tarah Locks concurrent Transactions ke conflicting operations ko control karte hain
aur problems jaise Lost Update ko prevent karne mein help kar sakte hain.


-------------------------------
## QUESTION: TYPE OF LOCKS ?
-------------------------------

1: SHARED LOCK (S LOCK)
------------------------

Shared Lock Read Operation ke liye use hota hai.

Multiple Concurrent Transactions ek hi data ko simultaneously 
Shared Lock ke saath read kar sakti hain.

Lekin jab Shared Lock active hota hai, to kisi doosri Transaction
ko usi data par conflicting Write Operation perform karne ke liye 
wait karna pad sakta hai, jab tak existing Shared Lock release nahi ho jaata.


### EXAMPLE
-------------

Maan lo:

Account A → Balance = ₹10,000

Transaction T1 Account A ka balance read karti hai:

T1 → Acquire Shared Lock
↓
Read Balance = ₹10,000

Isi time Transaction T2 bhi Account A ka balance read karna chahti hai:

T2 → Acquire Shared Lock
↓
Read Balance = ₹10,000

Dono Transactions ek saath read kar sakti hain:

T1 → READ ✅
T2 → READ ✅

Lekin ab Transaction T3 Account A ka balance update karna chahti hai:

T3 → UPDATE Account A
↓
Exclusive Lock Required
↓
WAIT

T3 ko generally tab tak wait karna padega jab tak existing Shared Locks release nahi ho jaate.


### CORE IDEA:
---------------

Multiple Reads Allowed, but conflicting Write has to wait.



2. EXCLUSIVE LOCK (X LOCK)
----------------------------

Exclusive Lock Write Operations ke liye use hota hai.

Agar kisi Data par kisi Transaction ne Exclusive Lock acquire kar rakha hai, 
to koi doosri Transaction usi Data par:

- Exclusive Lock acquire nahi kar sakti.
- Shared Lock bhi acquire nahi kar sakti.

Yaani Exclusive Lock ke dauran doosri Transactions ko conflicting Read 
ya Write Operations ke liye generally wait karna padta hai.


### IMPORTANT
---------------

However, agar kisi Data par kisi Transaction ne Shared Lock acquire kar rakha hai,
to doosri Transactions usi Data par aur Shared Locks acquire karke simultaneously
Read Operations perform kar sakti hain. Lekin koi Transaction us Data par Exclusive Lock
acquire nahi kar sakti jab tak existing Shared Locks release nahi ho jaate.


### EXAMPLE
-------------

Maan lo:

Account A → Balance = ₹10,000


Transaction T1 Account A ka balance update karna chahti hai:

T1 → Acquire Exclusive Lock
        ↓
     Update Balance
        ↓
     Lock Active


Isi time Transaction T2 Account A ko read karna chahti hai:

T2 → READ
        ↓
Shared Lock Required
        ↓
       WAIT


Isi time Transaction T3 bhi Account A ko update karna chahti hai:

T3 → UPDATE
        ↓
Exclusive Lock Required
        ↓
       WAIT


Jab T1 apna Transaction complete karke Lock release karegi:


Jab T1 apna Transaction complete karke Exclusive Lock release karti hai:

T1 → COMMIT / ROLLBACK
↓
Release Exclusive Lock
↓
Lock Manager waiting Transactions mein se
kisi eligible Transaction ko Lock grant karta hai.

T2 aur T3 ek saath proceed nahi kar sakte, kyunki:

T2 → Shared Lock Required

T3 → Exclusive Lock Required

aur Shared Lock aur Exclusive Lock ek hi Data par
simultaneously coexist nahi kar sakte.



-----------------------------------------
## QUESTION: LOCK GRANULARITY KYA HAI ?
-----------------------------------------

Lock Granularity batati hai ki Lock Data ke kitne bade portion par apply hua hai.

### EXAMPLE
-------------

Maan lo Transaction T1 Account A ka Balance update kar rahi hai.

Iske liye T1 ko Exclusive Lock acquire karna hoga.

Lekin Exclusive Lock exactly kis Data Unit par lagega?

- Sirf Account A ki Row par
- Ek Database Page par
- Puri Accounts Table par

Lock kis level ke Data Unit par apply hoga, isi ko Lock Granularity kehte hain.


## COMMON LOCK GRANULARITY LEVELS :
------------------------------------

Commonly Locks different levels par apply kiye ja sakte hain:

1. ROW-LEVEL LOCK
-------------------

Row-Level Lock mein Lock Table ki sirf ek specific Row par apply hota hai.

### EXAMPLE
-----------

Accounts Table:

Account A → ₹10,000
Account B → ₹5,000
Account C → ₹8,000

Transaction T1 Account A ko update karna chahti hai:

T1 → Acquires Exclusive Lock on Account A Row

Jab tak T1 ka Exclusive Lock active hai, doosri Transactions ko
Account A ki usi Row par conflicting Read/Write Operation perform
karne ke liye wait karna pad sakta hai.

Lekin Accounts Table ki doosri Rows, jaise Account B aur Account C,
is specific Row-Level Lock ki wajah se directly locked nahi hoti hain.


## BENEFIT
-----------

Sirf required Row ko lock karne ki wajah se, Table ki baaki Rows
unnecessarily block nahi hoti hain.

Isse generally higher Concurrency achieve ki ja sakti hai.


### LIMITATION
-----------------

Agar ek Transaction bahut zyada Rows par Locks acquire kare,
to Database ko large number of individual Locks manage karne pad sakte hain.

Isse Lock Management ka overhead increase ho sakta hai.


2. TABLE-LEVEL LOCK
---------------------

Table-Level Lock mein Lock complete Table par apply hota hai.

Ye generally tab useful hota hai jab Transaction ko Table ke bahut large
number of Rows par operation perform karna ho.

Aise case mein har Row par individually Lock acquire aur manage karna
expensive ho sakta hai, isliye Table-Level Lock use kiya ja sakta hai.


### EXAMPLE
------------

Maan lo `Accounts` Table mein millions of Rows hain:

Accounts Table:

Account A
Account B
Account C
...
Millions of Accounts

Ab Database ko `Accounts` Table par ek large-scale administrative operation,
jaise Table restructuring ya migration, perform karna hai.

Is operation ke dauran Transaction ko ensure karna ho sakta hai ki Table ka
data kisi doosri concurrent Transaction ke conflicting Read ya Write Operation ki wajah se
simultaneously modify na ho.

Iske liye:

T1:

Acquire Table-Level Lock
↓
Perform Required Table-Wide Operation
↓
Complete Operation
↓
Release Table-Level Lock

Jab Table-Level Lock active hota hai, to doosri Transactions ko Table par
conflicting Read ya Write Operations perform karne ke liye wait karna padta hai,
jab tak Lock release nahi ho jaata.


3. PAGE-LEVEL LOCK
--------------------

Database Data ko internally fixed-size storage blocks, yaani Pages, mein organize kar sakta hai.

Page-Level Lock mein Lock ek complete Page par apply hota hai.

Agar ek Page mein multiple Rows stored hain aur us Page par Lock acquire ho gaya,
to doosri Transactions ko us Page mein stored Rows par conflicting operations
perform karne ke liye wait karna pad sakta hai.

Page-Level Lock ki Granularity:

Row-Level Lock se larger
Table-Level Lock se smaller


4: DATABASE-LEVEL LOCK  
-------------------------

Database-Level Lock mein Lock poore Database par apply hota hai.

Yaani agar ek Transaction ke paas Database-Level Lock hai,
to doosri Transactions ko us Database ke data par conflicting operations
perform karne ke liye wait karna pad sakta hai.

### BENEFIT
-------------
Database ko bahut kam Locks manage karne padte hain.

### LIMITATION
----------------
Concurrency bahut significantly reduce ho jaati hai.

Isliye normal database Transactions ke liye Database-Level Lock
generally avoid kiya jaata hai aur ye relatively uncommon hai.



## GRANULARITY ORDER & CORE TRADE-OFF
---------------------------------------

Lock Granularity smallest se largest order mein:

Row-Level Lock
↓
Page-Level Lock
↓
Table-Level Lock
↓
Database-Level Lock


Jaise-jaise Lock Granularity smaller hoti hai:

Smaller Lock Granularity
↓
More Concurrency
↓
More Transactions simultaneously / concurrently execute kar sakti hain
↓
More Individual Locks to Manage
↓
Higher Lock Management Overhead


Jaise-jaise Lock Granularity larger hoti hai:

Larger Lock Granularity
↓
Less Concurrency
↓
More Transactions ko wait karna pad sakta hai
↓
Fewer Individual Locks to Manage
↓
Lower Lock Management Overhead


----------------------------------------------
## QUESTION: PESSIMISTIC LOCKING KYA HAI ?
----------------------------------------------

Pessimistic Locking approach mein jab kisi Transaction ko kisi Row ko
modify karna hota hai, to Transaction us Row par Exclusive Lock acquire karti hai.

Iske baad Transaction Row ko read karti hai, required changes karti hai
aur COMMIT / ROLLBACK karti hai.

Jab tak Exclusive Lock active hota hai, doosri concurrent Transactions ko
usi Row par conflicting operations perform karne ke liye wait karna padta hai,
jab tak Lock release nahi ho jaata.

Pessimistic Locking mein Transaction assume karti hai ki same Data par
concurrent conflict ho sakta hai.

### PESSIMISTIC LOCKING KA BENEFIT
------------------------------------

Conflicting Transactions ko pehle hi block karne ki wajah se
concurrency problems, jaise Lost Update, prevent kiye ja sakte hain.


### PESSIMISTIC LOCKING KA LIMITATION
---------------------------------------

Agar Lock long time tak hold rahe, to doosri Transactions ko wait karna padta hai.

Isse:

- Concurrency reduce ho sakti hai
- Request ka response time increase ho sakta hai


### COMMON USE CASE
---------------------

Pessimistic Locking generally un scenarios mein useful hoti hai
jahan same Row par frequently concurrent Updates hone ki possibility ho.




----------------------------------------------
## QUESTION: OPTIMISTIC LOCKING KYA HAI ?
----------------------------------------------

Optimistic Locking mein kisi Row ko Update karne ke liye pehle se Exclusive Lock
acquire karke doosri concurrent Transactions ko block nahi kiya jaata.

Is wajah se possibility hoti hai ki Transaction ke Data read karne ke baad 
koi doosri concurrent Transaction usi Row ko modify kar de.

Optimistic Locking generally assume karta hai ki Data Conflicts frequently nahi honge.


### QUESTION : VERSION KYA HOTA HAI ?
---------------------------------------

Optimistic Locking approach mein hum generally har Row ke corresponding Database mein 
ek Version Number maintain karte hain, jo track karta hai ki Row kitni baar successfully
update hui hai. Har successful Update ke baad Row ka Version Number generally increment 
kar diya jaata hai.


### QUESTION : HOW OPTIMISTIC LOCKING WORKS ?
----------------------------------------------

Transaction Row ko read karta hai and aur Row ka Version Number bhi 
read aur remember karta hai.

Iske baad Transaction required changes prepare karta hai aur apne changes
Database mein commit karne ka attempt karta hai.

Update attempt ke time Database check karta hai ki Row ka current Version
abhi bhi wahi hai ya nahi jo Transaction ne initially read kiya tha.


### QUESTION : DATABASE VERSION KAISE VERIFY KARTA HAI ?
---------------------------------------------------------

Transaction Row ko read karte waqt uska current version bhi read karti hai.

Update ke time wahi initially read kiya gaya version condition mein pass hota hai:

UPDATE accounts
SET balance = ?,
    version = version + 1
WHERE account_id = ?
  AND version = ?;

Last version = ? = Transaction ne initially jo version read kiya tha.

Affected Rows = 1 → Version same tha → Update successful.
Affected Rows = 0 → Version change ho chuka hai → Conflict detected → Retry/Fail.


### AGAR VERSION SAME HAI
----------------------------

Iska matlab hai ki Transaction ke Data read karne ke baad kisi doosri
concurrent Transaction ne us Row ko successfully modify nahi kiya.

Yaani Conflict nahi hua.

Is situation mein Transaction ke changes successfully update kiye jaate hain
aur Row ka Version Number generally increment kar diya jaata hai.


### AGAR VERSION CHANGE HO GAYA HAI
--------------------------------------

Iska matlab hai ki Transaction ke Data read karne ke baad kisi doosri
concurrent Transaction ne us Row ko successfully modify kar diya.

Yaani Concurrency Conflict detect ho gaya.

Is situation mein Transaction ka Update reject / fail kiya ja sakta hai
aur Application required action, jaise Transaction ko retry, kar sakti hai.



### OPTIMISTIC LOCKING KA BENEFIT
------------------------------------

Transaction ko row modify karne ke liye pehle se Exclusive Lock acquire karke
doosri concurrent Transactions ko block nahi karna padta.
Isse higher concurrency achieve ho sakti hai.


### OPTIMISTIC LOCKING KA LIMITATION
--------------------------------------

Agar same Row par frequently concurrent Updates ho rahe hain,
to Version Mismatch ki wajah se Conflicts frequently occur ho sakte hain.

Aise cases mein Transactions ke Updates repeatedly fail ho sakte hain
aur Transaction ko retry karna pad sakta hai.

Isse repeated retries ki wajah se unnecessary overhead aur performance
issues ho sakte hain.


### COMMON USE CASE
----------------------

Optimistic Locking generally un scenarios mein useful hoti hai
jahan same Row par concurrent Updates relatively rare hote hain.




