## QUESTION: TWO-PHASE LOCKING (2PL) KYA HAI?
---------------------------------------------

Two-Phase Locking (2PL) ek Concurrency Control Protocol hai jo define karta hai
ki ek Database Transaction Locks ko kis order mein Acquire aur Release karegi.

Ye concurrent Transactions ko safely coordinate karne mein help karta hai.

2PL ke according Transaction ka Locking behavior do phases mein divided hota hai:

1. Growing Phase
2. Shrinking Phase


1. GROWING PHASE
-----------------

Growing Phase mein Transaction required Locks acquire kar sakti hai.
Lekin is Phase mein Transaction koi acquired Lock release nahi kar sakti.


2. SHRINKING PHASE
-------------------

Transaction jaise hi Growing Phase mein acquired Locks mein se pehla Lock
release karti hai, woh Shrinking Phase mein enter kar jaati hai.

Lekin ek baar Transaction Shrinking Phase mein enter kar jaaye, uske baad
woh koi naya Lock acquire nahi kar sakti.

Shrinking Phase enter karte hi Transaction ko apne saare Locks ek saath
release karna zaroori nahi hai. Woh acquired Locks ko gradually release
kar sakti hai.

Eventually, Transaction complete hone par uske acquired Locks release ho
jaate hain.



## EXAMPLE
----------

Maan lo Transaction T1 ko Account A aur Account B update karne hain:

Acquire X Lock → Account A
↓
Acquire X Lock → Account B
↓
Update A and B
↓
Release Lock → Account A
↓
Release Lock → Account B

Ye 2PL follow karta hai kyunki Transaction ne pehle required Locks acquire kiye aur uske baad Locks release kiye.

Lock release karna start karne ke baad Transaction ne koi naya Lock acquire nahi kiya.



## LIMITATIONS
-----------------

Basic 2PL mein Transaction required Locks acquire karke Operations perform
karne ke baad, COMMIT / ROLLBACK se pehle bhi Locks release kar sakti hai.

Locks release karne ke baad Transaction eventually COMMIT ya ROLLBACK kar sakti hai.


Maan lo:

Initial State:

Account A = ₹1,000

T1:

Acquire X Lock on A
↓
Update A: ₹1,000 → ₹900
↓
Release X Lock on A
↓
T1 abhi COMMIT nahi hui


Ab agar Isolation Level Read Uncommitted ho, to T2 T1 ke
uncommitted changes ko read kar sakti hai:


T2:

Read A = ₹900
↓
Is value ke basis par apna operation perform karti hai

Ab T1 mein failure hota hai:

T1 → ROLLBACK

Account A:

₹900 → ₹1,000


## PROBLEM
------------

T2 ne ₹900 wali uncommitted value use ki, lekin baad mein T1 ka
Transaction rollback ho gaya.

Yaani T2 ne aisa Data read kiya jo eventually permanently commit hi nahi hua.

Ye Dirty Read problem hai.



------------------------------------------------
## QUESTION: STRICT TWO-PHASE LOCKING KYA HAI?
------------------------------------------------

Basic 2PL mein Transaction Locks ko COMMIT / ROLLBACK se pehle
release kar sakti hai.

Strict 2PL mein Transaction apne Exclusive Locks ko
COMMIT ya ROLLBACK hone tak hold karti hai.

Yaani:

Acquire Exclusive Lock
↓
Perform Operations
↓
COMMIT / ROLLBACK
↓
Release Exclusive Lock

Is duration mein doosri Transactions ko usi Data par conflicting
Read ya Write Operation ke liye wait karna padta hai.



---------------------------------
## QUESTION: DEADLOCK KYA HAI?
---------------------------------

Deadlock tab hota hai jab multiple Transactions ek doosre ke held Locks
ka wait kar rahi hoti hain aur Circular Wait create ho jaata hai.

Is situation mein koi bhi Transaction aage proceed nahi kar paati.


### EXAMPLE
------------

Maan lo 3 Account Rows hain:

Account A
Account B
Account C


Maan lo:

T1 ko Account A aur B update karne hain.
T2 ko Account B aur C update karne hain.
T3 ko Account C aur A update karne hain.


T1:

Acquire X Lock A
↓
Wait for Lock B

T2:

Acquire X Lock B
↓
Wait for Lock C

T3:

Acquire X Lock C
↓
Wait for Lock A


Final Situation:

T1 holds A → waits for B
T2 holds B → waits for C
T3 holds C → waits for A

Yaani:

T1 → T2 → T3 → T1

Ek Circular Wait create ho gaya.

Har Transaction kisi doosri Transaction ka Lock wait kar rahi hai, isliye koi bhi Transaction proceed nahi kar paati.

Is situation ko Deadlock kehte hain.



------------------------------------------------
## QUESTION: DEADLOCK DETECT KAISE HOTA HAI?
------------------------------------------------

Deadlock detect karne ke liye Database Wait-for Graph (WFG) use kar sakta hai.

Database internally track karta hai:

Kaunsi Transaction kaunsa Lock hold kar rahi hai.
Kaunsi Transaction kis Lock ke liye wait kar rahi hai.

Is information ke basis par Database Wait-for Graph banata hai.


## EXAMPLE
------------

T1:

- Lock A hold kar rahi hai.
- Lock B ke liye wait kar rahi hai, jo T2 ke paas hai.

Isliye:

T1 → T2

T2:

- Lock B hold kar rahi hai.
- Lock A ke liye wait kar rahi hai, jo T1 ke paas hai.

Isliye:

T2 → T1

Final:

T1 → T2
↑      ↓
└──────┘
CORE IDEA


Agar Wait-for Graph mein circular dependency / cycle detect hoti hai
to Database samajh jaata hai ki Deadlock occur ho gaya hai.



----------------------------------------------------
## QUESTION: DEADLOCK KO HANDLE KAISE KARTE HAIN?
----------------------------------------------------

Deadlock Handling ka matlab hai Deadlock detect hone ke baad
Database us situation ko resolve kaise karta hai.

Deadlock hone ke baad Database ko kisi tarah Circular Wait break karna hota hai.

Generally Database Deadlock mein involved Transactions mein se
ek Transaction ko Victim choose karta hai aur us Transaction ko
Rollback kar deta hai.

Victim Transaction Rollback
↓
Uske held Locks Release
↓
Waiting Transaction ko required Lock mil sakta hai
↓
Transaction aage Proceed kar sakti hai



--------------------------------------------
## QUESTION: DEADLOCK PREVENTION KYA HAI?
-------------------------------------------

Deadlock Prevention ka matlab hai Transactions ko aise rules follow karwana
jisse Circular Wait create hi na ho aur Deadlock occur na kar sake.


## COMMON APPROACH: CONSISTENT LOCK ORDER
-------------------------------------------

Consistent Lock Order mein multiple Resources / Rows ke liye ek predefined Lock 
Acquisition Order define kiya jaata hai, jise sabhi Transactions follow karti hain.

Maan lo do Account Rows hain:

- Account A
- Account B

T1 ko Account A aur B dono update karne hain.
T2 ko bhi Account A aur B dono update karne hain.


Ab rule define kar diya jaata hai ki agar Transaction ko Account A aur Account B 
dono ke Locks chahiye, to hamesha pehle Account A ka Lock acquire kiya jaayega 
aur uske baad Account B ka Lock.


CASE 1: WITHOUT CONSISTENT LOCK ORDER
---------------------------------------

T1 aur T2 dono ko Account A aur B update karne hain.

### T1
-------

Acquire X Lock on A
↓
Waits for X Lock on B

### T2
-------

Acquire X Lock on B
↓
Waits for X Lock on A


### FINAL SITUATION
---------------------

T1 holds A → waits for B

T2 holds B → waits for A

T1 → T2
↑      ↓
└──────┘

Circular Wait create ho gaya.

T1 aur T2 dono ek doosre ke held Lock ka wait kar rahi hain.

Is wajah se koi bhi Transaction aage proceed nahi kar sakti,
aur Deadlock occur ho jaata hai.**


CASE 2: WITH CONSISTENT LOCK ORDER
-------------------------------------

T1 aur T2 dono ko Account A aur B update karne hain.


## T1:
--------

Acquire X Lock on A
↓
Account A Locked by T1

Isi time T2 ko bhi Account A aur Account B update karne hain.

Rule ke according T2 ko bhi pehle Lock A acquire karna hoga.

## T2:
-------

Tries to acquire X Lock on A
↓
A already locked by T1
↓
WAIT

Account B available hone ke baad bhi T2 uska Lock pehle acquire nahi kar sakti,
kyunki predefined Lock Order follow karna hai:

A → B


### Meanwhile T1:
------------------

Already holds Lock A
↓
Acquire X Lock on B
↓
Perform Operations
↓
COMMIT / ROLLBACK
↓
Release Locks A and B


Ab:

T2 gets Lock A
↓
Acquire Lock B
↓
Perform Operations
↓
COMMIT / ROLLBACK
↓
Release Locks A and B


Is example mein T1 aur T2 dono ne same predefined Lock Order follow kiya:

Pehle Account A ka Lock → Phir Account B ka Lock

Isliye Circular Wait create nahi hua aur Deadlock prevent ho gaya.


